"Method":"SRB","DOI":"10.1016/j.jinorgbio.2014.08.005","Year":2014,"Type":"IC50","comparison":{"compound_id":"OBMD011700","measurement_id":"MCTM-036982","value":"8.10 ± 1.37","numeric":8.1}}],"logp":[{"abbreviation_in_the_article":"(3) [Pt(mal)(dbtp)2]","value":2.07,"value_relation":"=","type_authors":"logP","method":"shake-flask","aqueous_phase":"0.9% NaCl","doi":"10.1016/j.jinorgbio.2014.08.005","year":2014,"record_id":"MLDB-001973","detection":"UV-Vis","method_description":"The lipophilicity (log P) of the compounds was determined using the shake-flask method [30]. Aqueous (0.9% w/v) sodium chloride and organic (n-octanol) phases were saturated for 1 week. Drugs were dissolved at a concentration of 0.30 mg mL- 1 in 2.5 mL of saturated n-octanol, except for cisplatin and carboplatin, which were dissolved in the aqueous phase. An equal volume of immiscible solvent was added, and the solutions were mechanically mixed in plastic tubes for 24 h, followed by centrifugation (6000 rpm, 15 min). After separation, the phases were analyzed using UV–vis spectroscopy to determine the amount of the compound in each phase. The absorption values before and after shaking were compared. The partition coefficient in both phases for each compound was calculated based on the Lambert–Beer Law to determine the log P values. The procedures were repeated five times."}],"ccdc":["ZUJZAH"]}